package cajeta.io.net;

/**
 * The lifecycle states of a [`Server`](Server.cajeta) — the small,
 * monotonically-advancing state machine the accept loop and the
 * graceful-shutdown path coordinate on (plan item **NET-4.1**).
 *
 * ## The state machine
 *
 * ```
 *   NEW ──serve()──▶ RUNNING ──shutdown()──▶ DRAINING ──drained──▶ STOPPED
 * ```
 *
 * The transition is **one-way and monotonic** (the ordinals only
 * increase), so a single [`AtomicInt32`](../concurrent/AtomicInt32.cajeta)
 * holds the state and every transition is a `compareAndSet` from the
 * expected prior state. That gives two free invariants:
 *
 *   - **`serve()` is idempotent-guarded** — a second `serve()` finds the
 *     state already past `NEW` and refuses, so an accept loop is never
 *     started twice on one server.
 *   - **shutdown is a latch** — once `shutdown()` has moved the state to
 *     `DRAINING` (or beyond), the accept loop's `state == RUNNING` test
 *     fails and it stops pulling new connections; a second `shutdown()`
 *     is a no-op because the `RUNNING → DRAINING` CAS no longer fires.
 *
 * ## Why ordinals, not an enum type
 *
 * The states live as `int32` ordinals (the same posture
 * [`NetException.KIND_*`](NetException.cajeta) and
 * [`Reactor.READ`](reactor/Reactor.cajeta) take) so they drop straight
 * into the `AtomicInt32` cell the server's `compareAndSet` transitions
 * run against — no boxing, no separate enum-tag load. They are an
 * **append-only** contract: never renumber an existing value.
 */
public class ServerState {
    /**
     * Constructed but not yet serving — [`Server.bind`](Server.cajeta#bind)
     * leaves a fresh server here. The only legal next state is
     * [`RUNNING`](#RUNNING) via [`serve`](Server.cajeta#serve).
     */
    public static final int32 NEW = 0;

    /**
     * The accept loop is live: [`serve`](Server.cajeta#serve) has begun
     * pulling connections off the listener and dispatching them to the
     * handler. The accept loop continues while — and only while — the
     * server is in this state.
     */
    public static final int32 RUNNING = 1;

    /**
     * [`shutdown`](Server.cajeta#shutdown) has been requested: the accept
     * loop has stopped taking **new** connections, but in-flight handlers
     * are still being awaited (drained) up to the shutdown deadline. New
     * connections are refused; established ones run to completion.
     */
    public static final int32 DRAINING = 2;

    /**
     * Fully stopped: the accept loop has exited and every in-flight
     * connection has either completed or been force-closed at the drain
     * deadline. The listener is closed. Terminal — a `STOPPED` server is
     * not restartable (build a fresh one).
     */
    public static final int32 STOPPED = 3;
}
